In this lesson, we'll look at sample spaces, which is really just a fancy way of saying the complete list of everything that could happen in an experiment. The sample space is the set of all possible outcomes, and writing it out is the first step in almost every probability question. For example, if you toss a coin, your sample space is heads or tails. If you roll a standard die, it's the numbers one through six. If you draw a ball from a bag with three red and two blue balls, the sample space is just the colours, red or blue, since we only care about which colour came out. When you're listing outcomes, it's smart to be systematic so you don't miss anything. List them in order, and if there are two stages or two things happening, pop them into a table. If the experiment has clear step-by-step parts, a tree diagram works really well. Once your list is complete, counting your favourable outcomes is easy. Say you're rolling a die and you want an even number. Your favourable outcomes are two, four, and six, so that's three out of six. Now let's see it in action.